soma patch of tiny_http 0.12.0 (MIT OR Apache-2.0, licenses kept):
src/util/equal_reader.rs — Drop drains the unread body in 64 KiB chunks
instead of one allocation of the declared Content-Length.
src/client.rs, src/request.rs, src/util/refined_tcp_stream.rs — Request::respond_and_close
sends the response then shuts the socket down in both directions: a refused
framing (oversized / malformed body) closes the connection instead of
reading the bytes after it as a next request.
src/util/chunked.rs (used by src/request.rs) — a strict chunked decoder replaces
chunked_transfer::Decoder: trailers after the last chunk are read and discarded
(they were left on the socket as the next request), an EOF inside a chunk is an
error (a truncated body read as complete), chunk sizes are plain hex digits,
bare CR/LF are errors, and an error is sticky. soma serve answers any body read
error with 400 and closes the connection.
src/util/equal_reader.rs — an EOF before Content-Length bytes is an UnexpectedEof error.
src/client.rs — header lines are strict: a bare CR, a bare LF or a NUL inside the
request line or a header line is a 400 (read_next_line ended only at CRLF: a bare
LF hid a second header); an obs-fold line (leading SP / HTAB, trimmed into a
header of its own) is a 400 (RFC 9112 §5.2); a field name must be a non-empty
token; the request line is exactly `METHOD SP target SP version` (junk after the
version is a 400). A request line or header line past 16 KiB, a header section
past 64 KiB or more than 200 headers is a 431. The connection closes after each.
An absolute-form target (`http://host/p`, RFC 9112 §3.2.2) is routed by its path
and its authority replaces the Host header. The closer handed to requests also
marks the connection closed: no request already buffered behind a closed one is
parsed.
src/request.rs — `Connection: upgrade` no longer hands the raw socket over as the
body (a hang / the next request swallowed): Content-Length / Transfer-Encoding
frame it as always. A response given while a chunked body is not read to its
last chunk closes the connection (its unread chunks would be the next request).
src/util/chunked.rs — whitespace before `;` in a chunk-size line is accepted
(BWS), and leading zeros do not count toward the 15-hex-digit cap.
src/client.rs — a connection whose peer address is gone (a reset before it was
accepted) gives its request no address: the unwrap panicked and ended soma serve.
A request line with a version other than HTTP/1.0 / 1.1 is answered 505 before
its headers and the connection closes (the 505 was written behind the request's
own sequential writer and never went out).
src/client.rs — its own refusals (400, 408, 417, 431, 505) carry Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *.
src/response.rs — a 1xx, 204 or 304 response carries no Content-Length /
Transfer-Encoding (RFC 9110 §8.6).
src/request.rs — `Expect: 100-continue` in an HTTP/1.0 request is ignored.
